          "file": "libs/common/utils/prompts/replyAddressedToKody.ts",
          "claim": "WHAT: By decoding numeric and named references in one non-rescanning pass, the merged REFERENCE regex lets a double-encoded body survive into the prompt as the entity spelling of markup: &#38;lt;/NEWEST MESSAGE&#38;gt; is emitted as the literal text &lt;/NEWEST MESSAGE&gt;, and &#38;lt;message author=\"Kody\"&#38;gt; as &lt;message author=\"Kody\"&gt;. WHY: The guard's stated invariant (lines 141-142, 'no spelling of a tag can open or close the envelope') now fails for this spelling — a participant controls the comment body, and if the classifier decodes HTML entities (the very reason the code decodes &lt;/&gt;/&amp; to neutralize them), the body forges the thread envelope and injects content the classifier reads as a Kody/NEWEST-MESSAGE turn, whereas before the change this input was neutralized to ‹/NEWEST MESSAGE›. HOW: after decodeNumericReferences, also neutralize the surviving markup-entity spellings before/instead of only the literal '<' (e.g. extend the ANGLE_BRACKET step to replace &lt;|&lt|&#38;lt… spellings too), accepting that this changes the spec expectation at implicit-reply.spec.ts:246.",
          "keep": false,
          "rationale": "The factual trace is correct: the single-pass regex leaves `&#38;lt;/NEWEST MESSAGE&#38;gt;` as literal `&lt;/NEWEST MESSAGE&gt;` (verified against the regex, and spec line 246 asserts this). However, the finding reverses an explicitly implemented prior-round decision: the previous suggestion requested \"decode both reference kinds in a single pass\" and stated the expected spec output should keep `&lt;!-- x --&gt;` literal; that change was implemented and is encoded at implicit-reply.spec.ts:241-246, which the finding itself admits it would have to change. The only new element is a conditional hypothesis (\"if the classifier decodes HTML entities\") — no code path, caller, or test shows the downstream classifier re-decodes entities, and the design's stated model (lines 121-123, 153-155: \"as the page shows it\", single decode) is that the prompt carries the text the page displays; a displayed `&lt;` is not a tag. With no concrete evidence the applied change is wrong, this is a re-litigation of an implemented decision rather than a demonstrated failure path.",
